findings.body:
The React Scan loader logs when its dynamic import fails, but the TanStack loader's `.catch` returns a null component with no logging. If the devtools chunk fails to load in development, nothing appears in the console and the failure is invisible. Both loaders should handle failures the same way.

::diff{file="web/app/components/devtools/tanstack/loader.tsx" lines="6-12"}

The fix is to log the error the same way `react-scan/loader.tsx` does, while keeping the null fallback so the app still renders.

Neither loader has a test. The PR's main claims are that devtools render only when `IS_DEV` is true and that production never fetches their chunks. Both are untested, and a future refactor could move the `IS_DEV` check below the lazy boundary without any test failing.

A small spec per loader, mocking `@/config`, would cover the claims: with `IS_DEV` false the component renders nothing and the dynamic import is not invoked; with `IS_DEV` true it renders the wrapped component; and a rejected import falls back to null.

::code{file="web/app/components/devtools/tanstack/loader.tsx" lines="14-22"}

The description says `web/app/components/devtools/index.ts` was simplified to clean exports. No such file exists at the head commit, and the diff does not add one. The only consumers import the loaders directly by path (`layout.tsx` and `query-client.tsx`), which works. The description is inaccurate, and a reader expecting a barrel will be misled.

::diff{file="web/context/query-client.tsx" lines="1-6"}

Either add a barrel that re-exports both loaders, or drop the barrel claim from the description.
